## 3. Mandatory pre-render contract (per brief; no exceptions)
Before ANY billable call, record in the issue thread: (1) exact model id + endpoint + `X-Runway-Version: 2024-11-06`; (2) full parameters (ratio, duration/outputCount, reference count + SHA-256 of each anchor crop); (3) number of planned calls; (4) live cost estimate from §2 table + task `estimatedCost`; (5) funding check (creditBalance ≥ estimate; a gate approved against an unfunded pool 402s — proven on AAR-20); (6) brief approval reference; (7) `confirm_cost=true` set on the submission, gated behind an explicit human `confirm_cost` approval naming the exact contract and quote. **No billable generation call without an approved brief AND confirm_cost.** Moderation failures bill the same as successes — a rejected concept still costs its estimate, which is why Gate A runs first.

## 4. Multi-loop preflight (runs on paper, zero credits, before render)
Loop: **Gate A** prompt-vs-brief conflict scan (model/param compatibility vs §2 tables; negative block present and inside the prompt limit; forbidden-content cross-check prompt vs negative; moderation-risk flags) → **Gate B** continuity kit (approved anchor crop + SHA-256; identity/marker lock list; wardrobe clause from the AAR-20-revised standard) → **Gate C** scene hygiene (any visible text/letters/numbers, logos/brand marks/watermarks/stamps, UI/app frames/phone screens, signage/screens implied by the scene → surface exclusions added; disclosure badge plan exists for downstream composite). Run 1 → **consolidate ALL defects into ONE correction pass** (no per-defect regeneration; proven on AAR-14 where one pass cleared both variants) → Run 2 → Run 3 only if Run 2 introduced a NEW defect class → GO/NO-GO verdict recorded with the render contract. A NO-GO never spends.

Post-render QC (non-billable, on delivered pixels): 10 evenly spaced frames judged per frame (identity, wardrobe, zero-readable-text, anatomy), overlay badge re-verified on a frame decoded from the ENCODED file, phone-preview legibility gate — per AAR-14/AAR-11 standards.

